## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda environment
(`$GARS_PY` from `_system/gars-env.sh`), submitted as one batch job. Inputs are read-only;
nothing is written outside this analysis directory.

1. **Resolve inputs.** Run `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk
   --consumes counts_gene design` at job start and read the paths it returns; stop if
   resolution fails.
2. **Load and align.** Read the count matrix with pandas (tab-separated; `gene_id` as the index;
   drop the `gene_name` column). Read the design (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`). Require
   the matrix's sample columns and the design's `sample_id`s to be the same set (today: A1-A3,
   B1-B3); if they differ, stop with an error naming the mismatch. Order the matrix columns to
   match the design.
3. **Prepare counts.** The matrix is salmon length-scaled estimated counts (non-integer).
   Round to the nearest integer, which is how DESeq2-family tools take these values. Drop
   genes with a total count of zero across all samples.
4. **Variance-stabilise.** Apply the variance-stabilising transformation in PyDESeq2 0.5.4
   (`DeseqDataSet` with design `~1`, i.e. blind to condition, then `vst()`), giving
   log2-scale values with roughly constant variance across expression levels.
5. **Select genes.** Keep the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples in the
   VST values (the DESeq2 `plotPCA` default). The current matrix has 200 genes, so in practice
   every gene that remains after step 3 is used; the number used is written to the
   variance table.
6. **PCA.** scikit-learn 1.9.0 `PCA` on the samples × selected-genes matrix, centred
   (scikit-learn default) and not scaled to unit variance. Compute all components
   (n = number of samples − 1 = 5).
7. **Write tables.** `results/pca_scores.tsv`: one row per sample, columns `sample_id`,
   `condition`, `PC1` … `PC5`. `results/pca_variance_explained.tsv`: one row per component,
   columns `component`, `variance_explained_fraction`, `cumulative_fraction`, plus
   `n_genes_used`.
8. **Plot.** matplotlib 3.11.1: scatter of PC1 (x) vs PC2 (y), one point per sample,
   coloured by `condition` with a legend, each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis
   labels giving the percentage of variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (43.2%)`). Saved as
   `results/pca_pc1_pc2.png` (300 dpi) and `results/pca_pc1_pc2.pdf`.

